# Local credential store — TEMPLATE
#
# Copy to `.secrets.local` and fill in. That file is gitignored and must never
# be committed, shared, or pasted into a chat.
#
#     cp .secrets.local.example .secrets.local
#     chmod 600 .secrets.local
#
# Shell-sourceable (KEY=value, no spaces around `=`), so tooling can read it:
#
#     set -a; . ./.secrets.local; set +a
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY NOT client/.env.local or server/.env
# Those are APPLICATION config, loaded into the running app. This file is
# DEVELOPER TOOLING credentials — git pushes, MCP servers, one-off scripts.
# Keeping them apart means a tooling token is never accidentally read by the
# app, bundled by Next.js, or shipped to a hosting provider.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── GitHub ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used for `git push` and, if configured, the GitHub MCP server.
#
# Prefer a FINE-GRAINED token over a classic `ghp_` one: scope it to just this
# repository and just the permissions you need (Contents: read/write is enough
# to push; add Pull requests: read/write to open PRs).
#
#   https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens
#
# A classic token grants every scope you tick across EVERY repo on the account —
# far more blast radius than pushing one branch needs.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── Add others as needed ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Keep this file to tooling credentials only. Application secrets
# (MONGODB_URI, CLERK_SECRET_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY, …) belong in
# client/.env.local and server/.env.
